markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Building Regression Models

Lesson 5 opens with "Predict the Price": a ride-share service charges &#36;11 for 4 miles, &#36;15 for 6 miles, &#36;19 for 8 miles.

***Predict:*** How much would a 10-mile ride cost?

<div role="img" aria-label="Three ride cards: &#36;11.00 for 4 miles, &#36;15.00 for 6 miles, and &#36;19.00 for 8 miles" style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin: 20px 0; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center;">
  <div style="background: #FAFAFC; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; text-align: center; width: 210px;">
    <div style="display: inline-block; background: #E4E2F8; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; padding: 2px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px;">&#128663; Ride</div>
    <div style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; color: #1a1a1a;">&#36;11.00</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; margin-top: 8px;">&#8226; &#8226; 4 miles &#8226; &#8226;</div>
  </div>
  <div style="background: #FAFAFC; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; text-align: center; width: 210px;">
    <div style="display: inline-block; background: #E4E2F8; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; padding: 2px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px;">&#128663; Ride</div>
    <div style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; color: #1a1a1a;">&#36;15.00</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; margin-top: 8px;">&#8226; &#8226; 6 miles &#8226; &#8226;</div>
  </div>
  <div style="background: #FAFAFC; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; text-align: center; width: 210px;">
    <div style="display: inline-block; background: #E4E2F8; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; padding: 2px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px;">&#128663; Ride</div>
    <div style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; color: #1a1a1a;">&#36;19.00</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; margin-top: 8px;">&#8226; &#8226; 8 miles &#8226; &#8226;</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

To answer this, Lesson 5 teaches **linear regression**: finding a straight line through data like this, then using that line to make a prediction.

Here's the vocabulary you'll need along the way.


## Lesson Vocabulary


<div style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden; margin:20px 0;">
  <iframe
    src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/AIFY2/unit-2/regression-vocab-flip-cards.html"
    width="100%"
    height="500"
    frameborder="0"
    scrolling="no"
    title="Regression vocabulary flip cards: regression model, linear regression, slope, y-intercept">
  </iframe>
</div>


<div style="clear:both;"></div>

## Interpreting a Prediction Line

A separate example from the lesson shows how to actually read a prediction off a line: 
- locate Miles = 4 on the x-axis
- trace up to the line
- read across to find the cost, $9.

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px auto;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/0998a77b6f6cd420088a447b61f410a4-image-1794328966531.png" alt="A graph titled Interpreting a Prediction Line, with Miles on the x-axis and Cost in dollars on the y-axis, showing dashed lines reading a prediction of $9 at 4 miles" style="width: 55%; border-radius: 10px;">
</div>

## The Equation Behind the Line

Every prediction line follows the same formula, and that formula translates directly into Python.

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="line-height: 2;">y = mx + b</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 16px 0; display: flex; gap: 14px; align-items: flex-start;">
  <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; border-radius: 10px; width: 48px; height: 48px; min-width: 48px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 24px;">&#128208;</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.6;"><strong>Math connection:</strong> this is the slope-intercept form your students may know from algebra. Here the letters carry meaning: <strong>m</strong> is how much the price rises for each extra mile, and <strong>b</strong> is the starting fare. In the ride cards, every 2 extra miles adds &#36;4, so m is &#36;2 per mile, and a 0-mile ride would cost &#36;3, so b is &#36;3. That makes a 10-mile ride &#36;23. Try asking students to find m and b in the cards before showing the equation.</div>
</div>

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="line-height: 2;">m = 1.5</div>
    <div style="line-height: 2;">b = 3</div>
    <div style="height: 16px;"></div>
    <div style="line-height: 2;">def predict(x):</div>
    <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;return m * x + b</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> this is the exact shape every regression function in this unit takes, multiply the input by the slope, then add the y-intercept. You'll write a version of this yourself in the Python Lab.</div>
</div>



<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#128188; Career context:</strong> a Machine Learning Engineer writes code that uses data to build models capable of making useful predictions, exactly what students do in this lesson's Python implementation.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
